  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "The $6$th term of a geometric sequence is $972$, and the common ratio is $3.$ The first term of this sequence to exceed $30\\,000$ is the{{field-1}}th term.",
  "worked_solution": "Since we are given that ${a}_{6}=972$ and  $r=3$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{6-1} \\\\ 972 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{5} \\\\ 972 & =243{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{972}{243} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {3}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $30\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot {3}^{n-1} & >30\\,000 \\\\ {3}^{n-1} & >7\\,500 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}\u2061({3}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}\u2061(7\\,500) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}\u2061(3) & >\\operatorname{log}\u2061(7\\,500) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(7\\,500)}{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(3)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(7\\,500)}{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(3)}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(7\\,500)}{\\operatorname{log}\u2061(3)}+1\u22489.122.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $10$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $30\\,000.$",
